<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Naranjo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 500 BCE–950</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Wak Kab'nal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Naranjo, called Wak Kab'nal in Mayan, is a Maya city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peten">Petén Basin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, about 50 kilometres east of Tikal, occupied from about 500 BCE to 950 CE and at its height in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-classic-period">Late Classic period</a>; its rulers were the Holy Lords of Sa'aal. In 546 it came under the control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calakmul">Calakmul</a>, which in 682 sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lady-six-sky">Lady Six Sky</a>, a daughter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dos-pilas">Dos Pilas</a> ruler, to re-establish its dynasty; acting as ruler, possibly as regent for her son, she oversaw attacks that defeated Tikal in 695. Tikal defeated Naranjo in 744 and took its ruler captive. Its final abandonment may have followed political turmoil and a severe drought of 810, and the site has been severely looted.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naranjo.jpg" alt="Naranjo" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rudy Canales · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sitio_el_naranjo_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Eleven years later Yik'in Chan K'awiil defeated Waka' itself and converted it into a vassal state, carrying the image of its celestial serpent, possibly its patron deity, to Tikal and recording the triumph on Temple IV; <b>Naranjo</b> fell to him within the year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/el-peru-waka">El Perú-Waka' · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Yik'in Chan K'awiil defeated El Perú in 743 and <b>Naranjo</b> in 744, breaking the ring of Calakmul's allies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tikal">Tikal · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings traced their descent from founding ancestors, were enthroned in elaborate ceremonies and led in war and ritual, and royal women could hold power, as Lady Six Sky did at <b>Naranjo</b> in the late 7th century.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The most powerful took the title kaloomte' and ruled other kings as vassals: in the 7th century Yuknoom Ch’een II of Calakmul controlled the kings of Dos Pilas, Cancuén and <b>Naranjo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classic-maya">Social Hierarchy of the Classic Maya</a></li><li><blockquote>Allied with the Kingdom of Kaan, Caracol under Yajaw Te' K'inich II defeated Tikal in 562, and K'an II later fought Naranjo, recording his victories on monuments including a hieroglyphic stairway found at <b>Naranjo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/caracol">Caracol</a></li><li><blockquote>Its rulers fought frequently with Naranjo: in 710 <b>Naranjo</b>'s king K'ahk' Tiliw Chan Chaak claimed a victory over Yaxha and the scattering of a former Yaxha king's bones, and Naranjo attacked again in 799.</blockquote><a href="/state/yaxha">Yaxha</a></li></ul></section>
